\description{
Which features change most predictably over time, and how: q2-longitudinal's
\code{feature-volatility} pipeline. A random-forest regression of each sample's
state (time point) on its features ranks the features by importance; the
most important ones are summarized (their mean change between states,
global mean, spread and so on), and \code{plot()} draws the importances beside
the volatility chart of a chosen feature.
}
\details{
\strong{The pipeline}, as q2's:
\enumerate{
\item Hold out a random \code{test_size} of the samples (scikit-learn's
\code{train_test_split()}, not stratified).
\item With \code{rfe}, select features by recursive feature elimination on the
training samples (see below).
\item Grow a random forest of \code{n_trees} regression trees of the state on
the (selected) features of the training samples, and predict the
held-out samples' states, to see how well the features track time.
\item Rank the features by the forest's importance and keep the
\code{n_features} most important.
\item Summarize those features' relative abundances (of the whole table,
every sample): q2's \verb{_summarize_feature_stats()}. The mean at each
state is over every sample at that state, whatever its group.
\item For the chart, each feature's mean and bootstrap interval for each
group at each state, as \code{\link[=volatility]{volatility()}} computes them.
}

\strong{Recursive feature elimination} is scikit-learn's \code{RFECV} as q2 runs
it. The training samples are split, in order and without shuffling (they
are already in random order), into \code{rfe_folds} folds. In each, a forest
is grown on the other folds' samples and scored (\eqn{R^2}) on the fold;
the \code{rfe_step} least important features (impurity importance; ties in
the table's feature order) are dropped, and so on down to one feature.
The number of features with the largest total score over the folds (the
fewest, on ties) is kept: the same elimination on all the training
samples, stopped there, selects them. Every forest has the settings of
the final one. This grows about \eqn{(}\code{rfe_folds} \eqn{+ 1) / }
\code{rfe_step} forests (126 by default), so it takes about that many times
as long as \code{rfe = FALSE}.

\strong{Differences from q2-longitudinal}:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Random draws} (the split, the forest and the intervals) are
ecodive's own seeded PCG streams, not numpy's or JavaScript's, so they
differ from q2's as one \code{random_state} does from another. Given q2's
split and \code{random_state}, the selected features, the forest and its
importances are the same as q2's (see \code{\link[=random_forest]{random_forest()}}).
\item \strong{The forest's table} is relative abundances by default; q2 grows it
on the table it is given (usually counts): use \code{norm = 'none'} for that.
\item \strong{Features absent from every training sample} are kept, with
importance 0 (so recursive feature elimination drops them first, but
they count towards the fraction \code{rfe_step}); q2 drops them. Ties in
importance are ranked in the table's feature order.
\item \strong{\code{global_cv}} is the standard deviation over the mean, as q2's
"Global CV (\%)" column, which despite its name is not multiplied by
100.
\item q2's chart lets the viewer pick the feature and the grouping
interactively; here \code{plot()} draws one feature per call, and the
grouping is set by \code{groups}.
}
}
\section{Input Types}{


The \code{counts} parameter is designed to accept a simple numeric matrix, but
seamlessly supports objects from the following biological data packages:
\itemize{
\item \code{phyloseq}
\item \code{rbiom}
\item \code{SummarizedExperiment}
\item \code{TreeSummarizedExperiment}
}

For large datasets, standard matrix operations may be slow. See
\code{vignette('performance')} for details on using optimized formats
(e.g. sparse matrices) and parallel processing.
}

\section{Pseudocount}{

The \code{pseudocount} parameter is only relevant when \code{norm = 'clr'}
or \code{norm = 'log'}.

Zeros are undefined in the centered log-ratio (CLR) transformation and
in the log of proportions. If \code{norm} is \code{'clr'} or
\code{'log'}, \code{pseudocount} is \code{NULL} (the default), and
zeros are detected, the function uses half the minimum non-zero value
(\code{min(x[x>0]) / 2}) and issues a warning.

To suppress the warning, provide an explicit value (e.g., \code{1}), or
replace the zeros first with \code{\link{replace_zeros}()}, whose
result needs no pseudocount.

\strong{Why this matters:} The choice of pseudocount is not neutral; it acts as
a weighting factor that can significantly distort downstream results,
especially for sparse datasets. See Gloor et al. (2017) and Kaul et al.
(2017) for open-access discussions on the mathematical implications, or
Costea et al. (2014) for the impact on community clustering.

See \code{\link{aitchison}} for references.
}

\examples{
    # 8 subjects sampled on days 0, 7, 14 and 21; feature 1 rises.
    meta <- data.frame(
      subject = rep(sprintf('s\%d', 1:8), each = 4),
      day     = rep(c(0, 7, 14, 21), 8),
      diet    = rep(c('x', 'y'), each = 16) )
    counts <- outer(seq_len(32), 1:6, function (i, j) (i * j) \%\% 11 + 5)
    counts[, 1] <- counts[, 1] + 3 * meta$day
    colnames(counts) <- sprintf('F\%d', 1:6)

    fv <- feature_volatility(counts, 'subject', 'day', 'diet', data = meta,
                             n_trees = 20, cpus = 1)
    fv
    plot(fv)
}
